[Music]  this morning we're exploring an issue at  the center of the national political  debate the disturbing rise in murders in  many big cities  a recent study found homicide is up 37  in 20 u.s cities in chicago at least 497  people have been killed so far this year  that's according to the chicago's  tribune homicide tracker  that's already 145 more than last year  errol barnett looks at what may be  behind the rise in all these homicides  what have the past few months been like  for you  a tragic couple of months pastor donovan  price doesn't need statistics to tell  him  violence in chicago is up since the  pandemic began  you see he goes to crime scenes in his  underserved community to comfort  shattered families  often victims of random violence in your  view  why are things apparently getting worse  there's a lot of things that perhaps are  coming to head we were living in a war  zone before the pandemic  and so a pandemic on top of a war zone  on top of  of systemic issues is of course  a problem on top of a problem if we do  nothing  it seems to me we're in for several more  months  of elevated violence criminologist  richard rosenfeld is a professor at the  university of missouri st  louis he and his team recently released  this study on covert 19 and crime  it analyzed 11 criminal offenses in 27  cities and found during the pandemic  domestic violence and aggravated assault  rose  and homicides spiked 37 percent  from may to june with the most dramatic  increases in chicago  philadelphia and milwaukee in july the  chicago tribune reported at least  107 people were killed making it the  most  violent month there in nearly three  decades  one now has to speculate about why we  see  those increases it could be because  of the impact of the pandemic on police  activity  and also one has to consider the fact  that in many cities the police were  redeployed from their normal patrols to  address  protest activity  in late may protests began nationwide  against police brutality  following the death of george floyd  protests recently erupted in kenosha  wisconsin  over the police shooting of jacob blake  a recent poll found confidence in law  enforcement has hit a record low  u.s attorney general bill barr recently  tried to blame the crime uptick  on some protesters demands to defund the  police  i think it also is related to the  efforts that we've recently seen to  demonize  police and to defund their work the nypd  is  moving officers where they're needed  engaging with the community more deeply  to fight crime  increasing gun arrests in new york city  homicides in the last  month are up 50 percent compared to the  same time last year  and 34 year-to-date with 280 murders  so far in 2020. i think it's something  that we've ignored for a long time  gangs but the real problem in america  lies in handguns eric adams is the  brooklyn borough president and a former  nypd captain  he's calling for a multi-state gun task  force to address the violence  do we ask our police officers in general  to do too much  yes we do no matter what happens we  immediately want to call the police to  handle it  that is not the form of policing that we  need  we need to have greater crisis  management teams we need to have young  people be involved  in dealing with low-level nuisances  and then our faith-based institution we  must become more proactive  back in chicago pastor donovan is  grappling with the most  innocent victims of crime children this  is where it came out  that's where the bullet exited her body  out of the front and it entered through  her back  wow on june 30th pastor donovan was  there  minutes after three-year-old diana was  shot in her back  while playing outside her home diana  survived but no one has been arrested  for her shooting  deidre taylor is her mother do you  remember that day  you remember you got shot  what do you remember about how that felt  and what was happening around you  outside what would you say to people who  hear about these shootings  i was saying you're serious  it's no joke it's important to know that  more than a bullet gets penetrated  lives get penetrated and in this world  in this city  either part of the problem or you're  part of the solution right now  there's there's no in between for cbs  this morning  errol barnett new york  pastor donovan said something very  interesting there it's more than a  bullet that penetrates i know this a  three-year-old child should never be  asked  do you remember the day you got shot

